What BootForge does NOT contain
-------------------------------

BootForge ships no boot loaders, boot sectors, firmware drivers or other
binaries taken from other projects. The partition tables and the FAT32 and
exFAT file systems it writes are produced by its own code; the boot sectors
it writes contain only a tiny original "not bootable from this sector" stub.
Drives made in file-copy mode start through the UEFI boot loader that is
already inside the image you supply (EFI\BOOT\BOOT*.EFI); drives made in
image mode are byte-for-byte copies of your image.

Because of this, BootForge does not make BIOS/CSM-bootable drives from
non-hybrid ISO images and does not offer an NTFS + UEFI driver layout: both
would need third-party boot code.

Tools of the operating system
-----------------------------

To split a Windows install.wim that is larger than FAT32 allows, BootForge
runs the DISM tool that is part of Windows (%SystemRoot%\System32\Dism.exe)
on your PC. DISM is not distributed with BootForge.

Rust crates
-----------

BootForge is built with open-source Rust crates, among them eframe / egui,
image, rfd, serde, serde_json, the RustCrypto hash crates (sha2, sha1, md-5),
crc32fast, tiny-skia and embed-resource (build time) and the windows crate.
Each is used under its own licence (MIT and/or Apache-2.0 for most); see
Cargo.lock for the exact versions and each crate for its terms.

Test fixtures
-------------

tests/fixtures holds a few tiny optical-disc images used by the unit tests.
They were generated on Windows with its IMAPI2 file-system-image API from
invented files and contain no third-party software.

Fonts
-----

BootForge loads the Segoe UI and Segoe MDL2 Assets fonts that are installed
with Windows at run time. They are not distributed with BootForge.

